sequenceDiagram
  %% Container runtime (ADR 0250 step 5, docs/reference/development-containers.md#refresh-after-a-release): after each Gateway release, the deployment of each nested-fleet default Instance to the release commit. It is `instance.deploy` with the fleet steps that only a nested-fleet default's intent runs. It refreshes the inner fleet and takes the seed that new Instances of that Project clone. For the nested-fleet path it replaces the release trigger and the daily run of the task VM base refresh, which is removed.
  %% Decided by the owner (2026-11-15, ADR 0260 build step 4): when the proof Project's default records its seed, the Gateway copies it to each other Node that offers proof places (docs/cli/instance/seed-copy.mmd); a copy on a CI Node starts from that Node's seed.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: none · after each Gateway release · instance:deploy on the default runs the same flow
  %% kind: instance.deploy
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane C icon=server pill=default tone=purple
  %% lane F icon=server pill=fleet tone=gray
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>reached over ssh
  participant C as Container<br/>default Instance
  participant F as Inner fleet<br/>3 containers
  Note over GW: Admit nested-fleet refresh<br/>release commit
  %%> Started by:
  %%> - the verified release reaching fleet current, or Gateway verification when fleet rollout is disabled
  %%> When:
  %%> - only while the Gateway setting `ORBIT_INSTANCES_NESTED_FLEET_REFRESH` is on; off, the Gateway admits nothing
  %%> - one deployment per nested-fleet default whose runtime is in `orbit-instances-nested` on a Node with the nested-fleet switch on
  %%> - with no eligible default, the Gateway admits nothing
  %%> - a clone of the default is being created: refused, `instance.clone_in_flight`; the next release tries again
  %%> - a deployment of the default is open: refused by the Instance lock, `operation.target_busy`
  %%> - the release commit is missing from the Project's repository: the admission's source read refuses it, `project.source_unavailable`
  %%> Result:
  %%> - best effort: a refresh failure leaves the release live
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>instance-deploy-direct
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the intent carries `fleet` (NestedFleetIntent): the fleet's names, the operator, the checkout path and the egress proxy
  %%> - the run budget grows by the fleet steps' bounds
  SW->>N: Start the container<br/>Ansible · start · probe
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the start, then the read-only probe of the workspace; a checkout already past the release commit ends the run here as a no-op, `already_newer`, before the seed hold, `fleet_start`, the seed, the PHP and the bundle
  SW->>N: Prepare the container<br/>Ansible · fetch
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one play runs the phases `fleet_start` (with `fleet_room_check`), `seed` and `fetch`, in this order; a normal default runs only `fetch`
  %%> Why one step:
  %%> - none of these phases changes the workspace, so a failure in any of them restores nothing: they share their failure handling
  %%> - each Ansible step costs about 2.5 s to start its play: one step instead of four saves about 7.5 s of every refresh
  %%> On failure:
  %%> - the code and the detail name the failed phase, such as `instance.container_fleet_start_failed`, or the code is the phase's own refusal; nothing is restored
  %%> Phase start:
  %%> - the one start of a container; the Club key is delivered there, outside the container's disk
  %%> - a default frozen by a sleep resumes with its fleet, `wg-nested` and the Club key, through the power helper's `restore` (no plan: a nested-fleet sleep records none); swap closes on each cgroup of `limits.json` that still exists, and the asleep marker goes once the `start` part passed (the container runs and its units are active; no web probe); a stopped default boots as before
  %%> - the end of the deployment counts as activity: the idle sleep waits a full window (`deployed_recently`) before it freezes the default again
  %%> - the presented machine name: a stopped container gets `/etc/hostname`, `/etc/machine-info` and the `127.0.1.1` line of `/etc/hosts` through the file API before the boot, so the boot gives the kernel and T3 the name; a running or frozen container gets nothing, and nothing restarts
  %%> - grows the default's root disk to the intent's size (`ORBIT_INSTANCES_NESTED_DEFAULT_ROOT_SIZE`) before the start guard, online; never shrinks it
  %%> Phase fleet_start:
  %%> - first reads the room on the nested Nodes' disk (`df` in the container of `/`, or of the inner volume's mount when the default has one: where the nested Nodes' `/` lies): the inner Doctor wants at least 2 GiB and 10% of it free (`node.disk_low`), and a refresh writes up to 2 GiB more (checkout, installs, the nested Gateway's records, and a `base` retake); it refuses `instance.container_fleet_no_room` when the free space minus 2 GiB is under that floor, and the detail names the size, the free space, the floor and the need
  %%> - the one fleet check of the run: waits for the inner Incus, compares the three nested containers with the fleet rules, starts a nested container that is stopped, links the Club key and brings up `wg-nested` with `ip` and `wg`
  %%> - appends orbit to incus-admin on nested Nodes marked as Instance hosts; preserves other groups, skips non-hosts, and reports changed only for new membership; every copy start repairs this without a converge or seed refresh
  %%> - reads the nested Gateway's command list as orbit; if `gateway:known-hosts:sync` exists, restores SSH known_hosts from recorded Node pins; otherwise records `command_unavailable` and continues on the old checkout
  %%> - gives `wg-nested` the hub's resolver `10.45.0.1` for the routing domains `~nested-dev` and `~nested-prod` with `resolvectl`, never `~orbit`, setting only what differs (docs/reference/development-containers.md#the-fleet-step)
  %%> - checks only what a start can promise: the three containers run and the tunnel reaches the hub; no Doctor wait
  %%> - changes no device or configuration key: on a running fleet it only reads
  %%> - reads no fleet file: every file rule is owned and never refuses; the refresh reads the files when they matter
  %%> - refuses `instance.container_fleet_drift` when a rule Orbit does not write differs (`raw.idmap`), or a nested container or the inner bridge cannot be read; the Operation's result `fleet_drift` names each difference
  %%> - an owned rule that differs or a build rule that is absent passes because this deployment follows with refresh; the refresh writes it with the fleet stopped before retaking `base`
  %%> - a build rule with a conflicting value still refuses; a standalone start or proof reset also refuses an absent build rule
  %%> - after a later failure, deployment rollback also admits absent build rules when it resumes the restored seed; it changes no map and leaves the upgrade to the next deployment
  %%> fleet_start · why:
  %%> - the fleet keeps running between releases, so the run checks it once, before it changes anything; a refusal or a failed start restores no seed
  %%> - a refresh that cannot end above the inner Doctor's floor would fail only at its last readiness wait, after minutes of work and a restore: refresh 21617 did that on `node.disk_low`
  %%> - nothing in the run stops the fleet before the refresh, so no second start follows the checkout
  %%> - it takes the hub's key only when it is a WireGuard key: just after a boot, `wg` prints `(none)` with exit 0 until `wg-quick` has set the key
  %%> fleet_start · on failure:
  %%> - no key within 60 s: the step says the nested Gateway's WireGuard key was not ready, and sets no peer
  %%> Phase seed:
  %%> - retakes `seed-COMMIT` of the default as it is, fleet included, before the run changes anything
  %%> - the proof Project's default holds its seed first, in the step "Hold the seed" before this step, so no seed copy pushes the seed while it is retaken (instance/seed-copy.mmd, container/deploy-default.mmd)
  %%> - takes the new snapshot first, as `next-COMMIT` beside the old `seed-COMMIT`; only then does the old one go and the new one take its name (docs/reference/development-containers.md#a-retake-keeps-the-seed)
  %%> - refuses `instance.fleet_key_on_disk` first when the Club key path is a file on the container's disk; the key is never read
  %%> seed · on failure:
  %%> - a snapshot Incus cannot take, such as one with no free isolated ID range: `instance.container_seed_failed`, and the error detail names why; the old seed stays, nothing else changed, and the run fails with no restore
  %%> seed · why:
  %%> - a failed refresh then restores a seed that holds the fleet, never one from before it
  %%> Phase fetch:
  %%> - fetches into the Node's mirror and pushes the branch into the container as a bundle; a pinned commit off the branch's first-parent chain refuses `deployment.commit_not_on_branch`
  SW->>C: Check out in the container<br/>Ansible · tags checkout
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - checks out the release commit in `/srv/workspace`; the nested Gateway and app-dev see it through their `checkout` device
  SW->>C: Run dev deploy steps<br/>incus exec · vault copy
  SW->>C: Finish the container<br/>Ansible · processes · php_index · seed · check
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one play runs the phases `processes`, `fleet_refresh`, `php_index`, `seed` and `check`, in this order; a normal default runs `processes`, `php_index`, `seed` and `check`; `php_index` does work only for a Laravel default (container/deploy-default.mmd)
  %%> - the handlers a phase notifies run before the next phase starts, as with separate plays
  %%> Why one step:
  %%> - every one of these phases runs after the checkout, so a failure in any of them restores the old seed: they share their failure handling
  %%> - each Ansible step costs about 2.5 s to start its play: one step instead of four saves about 7.5 s of every refresh
  %%> On failure:
  %%> - the code and the detail name the failed phase, such as `instance.container_fleet_refresh_failed`, or the code is the phase's own refusal; the run restores the old seed
  %%> Phase processes:
  %%> - installs the new and changed Process units and waits for each running unit
  %%> Phase fleet_refresh:
  %%> - after migrating to the new checkout, retries the same command check and SSH pin sync, even when the code digest is unchanged; pins are written before readiness and base
  %%> - reads the refresh record in the container (`/var/lib/orbit/fleet-refresh.json`) and the digest `code` of the checkout: `apps/gateway`, which holds the migrations and the Composer lock of packages that ship migrations, and `apps/agent/contract`, which it reads
  %%> - after the migrate, makes one converge digest per nested Node from what its default converge reads: the `swamp` files that `swamp/ansible/fleet-converge-inputs.json` lists, the role, software, Tool and network includes too, the nested Gateway's digest of that Node's default intent (`orbit:node-converge-digest`, which admits and writes nothing) and the fleet intent
  %%> - for the eligible `nested-app-dev` Instance host, compares a separate digest of the rendered host intent, the machine-software recipe and the `swamp/ansible` tree except the three Swamp role files and two Conn controller files named in docs/reference/development-containers.md#instance-host-in-the-fleet; all other paths, including new paths, count
  %%> - a missing host digest or a failed read of a required host input makes the host due without recording a partial digest; the five host exclusions leave grants and every other Node's inputs unchanged
  %%> - a part runs when its digest differs from the record; a missing record, or one it cannot read, makes every part run, and an unreadable record adds a warning
  %%> - a Node also converges when its last converge (`converged_<role>` in the record) is more than 7 days old, when the record holds no digest for it, or when its own digest cannot be read; one Node's unreadable digest does not make the others due
  %%> - each Node has its own reason: `digest_unreadable` (with the error), `no_record`, `digest_changed`, `stale`, `host_due` (nested-app-dev only: its base digest did not change, but the Instance host part is due) or `unchanged`; `unchanged` alone means the Node does not converge
  %%> - also from the record: `grants` (with the Club key only), the digest of `apps/gateway` and `swamp`, from which the nested Gateway renders its Swamp grants; `tokens`, the creation time of the oldest of the nested Gateway's six Swamp tokens
  %%> - every run, once: waits until the state mounts of the nested Gateway (with its Swamp state when the Club key is there) and app-dev are active (60 s)
  %%> - `code` changed: places the agent delivery (into the checkout)
  %%> - `grants` changed, with the Club key: reloads the grants (on the nested Gateway's own disk)
  %%> - with the Club key, when `tokens` is missing or older than 20 days (`fleet_token_max_age`): reads the nested Gateway's Swamp tokens (`swamp access token list`, names and dates only); they are due when one of the six (serve driver, Gateway run and read, the access of workers a, b and c) is missing or older than 20 days
  %%> - every run, with the Club key: reads the checkout recipe's Swamp release and sha256 and the binary that the nested Gateway's serve runs; when serve does not run that release at the fleet build's path `/usr/local/lib/orbit-swamp/<sha256>/swamp`, or a read fails, the `swamp-pin` play of the nested Gateway installs it by checksum through the egress proxy, stops the workers and serve, links the new binary, starts serve, waits for `/ready`, starts the workers, checks that serve and each worker run it, and then removes the other releases; the fleet build installs Swamp once, so a pin moved by a release reaches the fleet here; it marks the nested Gateway for `base`
  %%> - the tokens are due: the `remint` play stops the workers and serve, makes the worker set whole (folder and tokens of a missing worker), renews all six, and enables and starts serve and the three workers; the step ends only when serve and the three workers are active, so nothing else of the refresh runs on them meanwhile; a remint marks the nested Gateway for `base` and restarts its services, which read the new tokens
  %%> - `code` changed: migrates the nested Gateway and restarts its services
  %%> - every run: reads the fleet rules on the running fleet, its devices and keys and the inner bridge; reads the fleet files only when `rules` (the files' content) differs from the record or the converge is due, and writes those that differ
  %%> - every run: checks the services of the nested Gateway, and Swamp serve and its workers with the Club key, in one read
  %%> - a converge is due: waits until the three agents are online (300 s), converges each due nested Node with the default scope (base, software, recorded roles, Tools, network): the nested Gateway first and alone, then `nested-app-dev` and `nested-app-prod` side by side; it admits both app Nodes together, reads their Operations in one call every 5 s until both have ended, and reads how many tasks each changed; a failed or refused converge fails the refresh after its group has ended and names each Node that failed; a failed nested Gateway admits no app Node; below the 2,000,000 ID map size `nested-app-dev` converges with the base scope
  %%> - every run: waits for readiness once (Doctor healthy, three agents, the operator), so the seed holds a ready fleet: before `base` and the tools when the fleet keeps running, after the start that follows a stopped `base` otherwise
  %%> - retakes `base` only for the containers whose disk changed: a step that writes a nested disk and reported a change (the grants reload that changed a file, a remint, a Swamp install), that container; a converge that changed a Node, that Node and the nested Gateway; a missing `base`, that container; a missing or unreadable record or a rule or file that differed, all three; a shrink that ran after the last `base` (`shrunk`), all three; a due shrink adds none
  %%> - every run, before `base` is worked out: checks whether the nested root disks need their shrink: due when no shrink is recorded or a nested container's dpkg status changed since the last one (under a second); `shrunk` when a shrink ran after the last `base`; a check that cannot read is not due
  %%> - every run, before its first write to a nested disk (before the record goes): stops `orbit-fleet-shrink` when it still runs, so no shrink runs while a refresh holds `base`; a stopped shrink writes no record and is due again
  %%> - a due shrink starts after `base` and the tools, as the transient unit `orbit-fleet-shrink` in the Instance container (900 s), and runs on after the refresh while the three run: it links the files that rust-coreutils ships as links of `/usr/bin/coreutils` again (only those with that file's dpkg digest and the same bytes, owner and mode, through folders held open without following a symlink), removes the package files in each nested container's apt archives (following no symlink), then `duperemove -dr` over the three root disks shares identical blocks; it writes its record with `base: false`, and none when a dpkg status changed during the run or `duperemove` is missing; the result says `shrink: started`, or `failed` when the unit did not start; nothing about the shrink fails the refresh
  %%> - after a `base` of all three for `shrunk`: records that `base` holds the shrink, so the next check is current
  %%> - on a btrfs inner pool with no rule or file that differed: after the run's one readiness wait, deletes the old `base`, holds the nested Gateway's Valkey writes (`CLIENT PAUSE 30000 WRITE`, which ends by itself) and saves its queues (`valkey-cli SAVE`; a failed hold or save lets the writes go and takes the stopped path), arms a 60 s thaw timer in the Instance container (`orbit-fleet-thaw`, which resumes the targets if the controller is lost), refuses a hold with under 10 s left (writes go, timer cancelled, stopped path), freezes the targets in one `incus pause` bounded by the hold's time left minus 5 s, refuses a freeze that failed, ran out or left under 5 s of the hold (targets resumed, writes go, timer cancelled, stopped path), snapshots each, thaws them in one `incus resume`, also when a snapshot fails, cancels the timer and lets the Valkey writes go (`CLIENT UNPAUSE`); nothing stops or starts; the result says `base_mode: running`
  %%> - otherwise (another pool, unreadable pools, a rule or file that differed) each of them stops first; the others keep running; with all three stopped it writes the owned rules (and a build rule only where it is absent), and it checks every rule; with some stopped the rules only compare, and a difference fails the refresh; the result says `base_mode: stopped`
  %%> - a run that retakes `base` but did not read the fleet files, or whose converge ran after it read them, reads them first, on the running fleet; a file that differs makes `base` due for all three
  %%> - after a stopped `base`, starts the containers it stopped and waits for readiness, its one readiness wait in that case, so the seed holds a running, ready fleet
  %%> - otherwise the fleet keeps running and `base` stays
  %%> - every run, after `base`: keeps the pinned shape tools in the Instance container at `/opt/orbit-shape-gate`, named by the checkout's own `bin/swamp-shape-gate` from its three pin files and, for the venv, the interpreter that builds it; when the venv and the collections of these pins are complete it only reads them; a package of its six (`python3-venv`, `zip`, `unzip`, `jq`, `btrfs-progs`, `duperemove`) that is missing beside ready tools it installs with one `apt-get install`; otherwise it builds them and removes the tools of other pins or another interpreter
  %%> - removes the record before its first write to a nested disk and writes it again last, so a run that stops in the middle leaves none, and the next run does every part and retakes `base` of all three
  %%> - the result names each part, ran or skipped, why each Node converged or not (`converge_reasons`, with the phases each converge ran), the tasks the converges changed, what it did with the tokens and why (`tokens`, `tokens_reason`), what it did with Swamp and why, and the release it runs (`swamp`, `swamp_reason`, `swamp_version`), what it did with the shape tools and why (`tools`, `tools_reason`), what it did with the shrink and why (`shrink`, `shrink_reason`), `base` retaken or kept and for which containers, what it did with the Instance host (`host`: `converged`, `kept` or `skipped_idmap_small`; the Operation leaves it out when the run names none or another word), and the seconds of each part
  %%> - every API call goes from the container over `wg-nested`, never through the container's egress proxy; a failed Doctor wait names the last status and every issue of the last body (code, Node and summary, each bounded), or curl's error
  %%> fleet_refresh · why:
  %%> - most releases change nothing the nested Nodes hold: stopping the fleet and copying `base` three times cost about 3 minutes, and three base converges that change nothing about 87 s
  %%> - `base` holds Nodes converged with the default scope at the seed's commit, so a proof's converge or role-add case after a reset finds nothing to change unless the commit or the case changed an input; with a base converge, every such case installed the software and the role state again
  %%> - a file list cannot follow the Gateway code that builds an intent, so the nested Gateway hashes the intent itself; the `swamp` list has a cover test
  %%> - the list leaves out the imports of the behavior file that a converge never runs (the fleet reboot's result, role plans, the clone part): a change there converged the three Nodes for nothing twice on 14 November 2026 (108 s and 120 s)
  %%> - one digest per Node: a moved intent converges that Node only
  %%> - a Swamp install that fails, or a serve that does not come back on the new binary, fails the step: the run restores the old seed, and with it the old binary and link, because they live on the nested Gateway's own disk inside the seed
  %%> - the seed is retaken after the readiness wait (Doctor healthy), so a copy from it starts its inner serve on the new binary, with the Club key mounted from tmpfs as before
  %%> - the nested Gateway's Swamp tokens live 30 days, and a fleet lives longer: the refresh keeps them young, so no copy stops with expired tokens; an older fleet with only workers a and b gets worker c first, because the remint renews all three
  %%> - the nested Gateway converges first: its Node is the hub, and the nested Gateway refuses a converge of the hub with roles beside any other converge; it also runs the Swamp workers that run the other converges
  %%> - the app Nodes converge side by side: two peers of the hub pass each other's reservation, so the converge takes the longer of the two instead of their sum (2162: 216 s and 330 s one after the other)
  %%> - a reset restores `base` of all three in seconds; taking `base` costs about 32 s per container, so only what changed is retaken
  %%> - a migration does not retake `base`: a reset restores `base` and then migrates the nested Gateway forward to its checkout, and restarts it when one applied; a retake for each migrating release cost 95 to 100 s (21810, 21978)
  %%> - the grants, the agent delivery, the token read and the file reads cost about 8 s of every run, and a docs-only release changes none of their inputs
  %%> - the set stays consistent: the nested Gateway's database holds the schema and records each converge, so its `base` is retaken with any other
  %%> - a btrfs snapshot of a running container is atomic, and the freeze gives the set one point in time: the stop, the second rules pass, the start and the second readiness wait cost 37 s of a live refresh (28507); a reset clears the network pid files of a running `base` after its restore
  %%> - a snapshot of a running container on a directory pool is a file copy, not a consistent state, so there `base` is taken with the fleet stopped
  %%> - the shrink runs after the refresh, on the running fleet: as a step of `base` it forced the stopped path, and the refresh of Instance 2454 after release 2162 spent 163 s in `base` instead of about 10 s, at every release that changes a nested package; the relink and the dedupe are safe on running root disks (atomic swap of a link with an identical file, swapped back when a package replaced it, held folders, the kernel compares before it shares), a seed taken during the shrink holds whole files and the earlier `base`, so a seed copy never pushes a half-written `base`; the next refresh retakes `base` so it holds the small form; the first nested fleet held 116 separate copies of rust-coreutils' one binary in each machine and shared no blocks between the three; the shrink took its inner pool from 11.8 GiB to 3.7 GiB in 36 s
  %%> - the proof runner in a copy needs the pinned Ansible controller, and the default is never rebuilt from a new image: the seed after this step gives every copy the tools of its own commit, so no copy builds them or needs `python3-venv`
  %%> fleet_refresh · on failure:
  %%> - the start after `base` fails: the refresh fails, and the run restores the old seed; the new `base` snapshots are inside the container's disk, so the restore takes them back too; the default then runs on the old seed's commit and fleet; existing copies are clones of older seeds and stay as they are; new copies clone the restored seed until the next release
  %%> - a failed tools install does not fail the refresh: the result says `tools: failed` with the step in `tools_reason`, the log shows the end of its error output, and the seed is taken without ready tools (the venv's `.complete` is written last); a proof in such a copy builds the tools in its own cache, and when that fails its fleet preparation fails with the line that names the missing pinned folder
  %%> Phase seed:
  %%> - refuses `instance.fleet_key_on_disk` when the Club key path is a file on the container's disk; the key is never read
  %%> - stops the Processes, snapshots `seed-COMMIT` again even at the same commit, starts them, deletes the older seeds
  %%> - at the same commit the new snapshot is `next-COMMIT` until it exists; then the old `seed-COMMIT` goes and the new one takes its name: never `--reuse`, which deletes first
  %%> - a default with an inner volume also snapshots the volume under the same name with the Processes stopped; the pair is the seed, and the older pair goes only after both new halves exist
  %%> - right before the volume's half: `sync -f /srv/inner-pool` as root in the container, so the block snapshot holds what btrfs has not committed yet (it commits every 30 s); no freeze: the halves are separate snapshots, so a freeze adds nothing over the flush
  %%> - a failed half or flush deletes only the new halves and fails `instance.container_seed_failed`, `instance.seed_volume_flush_failed` or `instance.seed_volume_snapshot_failed` with the cause in the detail; the old seed stays
  %%> - the inner fleet runs during the snapshot, also after a retaken `base`: on the directory pool the inner pool lies on the container's disk, so the seed is one point in time of the whole fleet, as after a power cut; on an inner volume the two halves are taken one after the other, apart by the flush: seconds at most under load
  %%> - the seed holds the refresh record of the fleet it holds
  %%> Phase check:
  %%> - the branch is at the release commit and the units are active
  alt every step passes
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    Note over GW: Record the seed commit
    %%> Result:
    %%> - new Instances of the Project clone the new seed; existing clones keep the fleet they have
    opt default of the proof Project
      Note over GW: Admit the seed copy<br/>instance.seed_copy
      %%> When:
      %%> - one Node or more that offers proof places, other than the default's Node, has a seed that is not this new seed (docs/reference/development-containers.md#the-seed-on-each-ci-node)
      %%> - a seed copy is open: refused by its lock, `operation.target_busy`; the open copy admits the next one when it ends
      %%> Why:
      %%> - each CI Node keeps its own seed, so a proof copy there starts from its own Node; one refresh makes one fleet, and the copy moves it once per seed and Node
      %%> Result:
      %%> - best effort: the refresh has ended, and nothing in the release waits for the copy
    end
  else a step fails from the checkout on
    SW->>N: Restore the old seed<br/>Ansible · tags fleet_restore
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - stops the container and restores its newest seed by creation time: the one the `seed` phase of "Prepare the container" took, or a newer one
    %%> - a default with an inner volume restores the volume to its snapshot of the same name too; a failed volume restore fails the run with the container already restored
    %%> - refuses `instance.fleet_seed_missing` when no seed is left, or when the inner volume lacks the seed's half; both are checked before anything is restored
    SW->>N: Start on the old seed<br/>Ansible · tags start
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - starts it through `start`, so the start delivers what every start delivers
    SW->>F: Start the restored fleet<br/>Ansible · tags fleet_resume
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - starts the inner fleet of the restored seed and waits for readiness, so the default is as it was before the run
    %%> - on failure the error detail adds: the inner fleet of the restored seed did not start, and why
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the Operation names the first failed step and its code, such as `instance.container_fleet_refresh_failed`
    %%> - a fleet phase's error detail names the failed task and its message, such as the Doctor body
    %%> - the old seed stays the seed; clones keep copying it, and the next release runs again
    %%> - the release has ended: the Activity row `instance.refresh_failed` (status failed, command `instances:refresh-nested-fleets`) names the Operation, its code and its detail, so `activity:list` and the problem collector show it
  end
